From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 13 Aug 2026 21:30:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] Keep Emu68's own memory out of the range given to the guest

Emu68 carves its allocator and JIT buffers out of DRAM and then hands the
guest a /memory node describing all of it. The guest builds its heap from
that range and the two allocators proceed to hand out the same addresses.

Measured on a standalone build, 2026-08-13: the SYS pool ran
0x0088a000-0x01ffffff while the AROS heap began at 0x01000000, so sixteen
megabytes had two independent owners. It surfaced as free-list corruption
inside the guest's own allocator -- free_node pointers reading back as
0x000c2c6f and 0x00104eae, values that are data and not addresses -- in a
block sitting inside the overlap. It had been attributed to a wild pointer in
the guest for a long time. There was no wild pointer; the memory had another
owner the whole time.

The fix is half of a loop that already exists. The block that corrects the
guest's copy of /memory already trims anything above top_of_ram -- the loaded
ELF, the device tree copy itself. It never trimmed the bottom, where Emu68's
own pool is. Now it does, using the same range pointer and the same cell
writes, so a guest that reads its memory range is simply right and needs no
change of its own.

Three earlier attempts published the extent as a property on /emu68 instead,
and none of them arrived. dt_add_property() edits Emu68's in-memory tree,
while the guest receives `memcpy(fdt, dt_fdt_base(), dt_total_size())` -- a
byte copy of the original blob, which has no /emu68 node at all. Correcting
/memory is both the mechanism that already reaches the guest and the one that
needs no cooperation from it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/src/aarch64/start.c
+++ b/src/aarch64/start.c
@@ -1630,7 +1630,12 @@
             int block_size = (size_cells + address_cells);
             int block_count = p->op_length / (4 * block_size);
 
-            kprintf("[BOOT] Adjusting memory blocks\n");
+            uintptr_t host_mem_end =
+                ((uintptr_t)boot + (KERNEL_SYS_PAGES << 21)) & ~((1 << 21) - 1)
+                & 0xffffffff;
+
+            kprintf("[BOOT] Adjusting memory blocks (Emu68 keeps up to %p)\n",
+                    host_mem_end);
             for (int b = 0; b < block_count; b++)
             {
                 uintptr_t base = 0;
@@ -1646,6 +1651,63 @@
                 }
 
                 kprintf("[BOOT]   %p - %p ", base, base + size - 1);
+
+                /*
+                    Trim the bottom as well as the top.
+                
+                    This loop already excludes what sits above top_of_ram --
+                    the loaded ELF, this device tree copy. What sits *below*
+                    the guest's memory is Emu68's own: the SYS pool runs from
+                    __bootstrap_end to kernel_top_virt, and it was advertised
+                    to the guest as ordinary memory.
+                
+                    Measured on 2026-08-13: the pool ran 0x0088a000-0x01ffffff
+                    and the AROS heap began at 0x01000000, so sixteen megabytes
+                    had two independent allocators handing out the same
+                    addresses. It surfaced as free-list corruption inside the
+                    guest's allocator, whose free_node pointers came back
+                    holding data rather than addresses -- and as a boot failure
+                    long attributed to a wild pointer in the guest. There was
+                    no wild pointer. The memory had another owner.
+                
+                    Excluded here rather than announced elsewhere, because this
+                    is where the guest's own copy of /memory is already being
+                    corrected, and a guest that reads its memory range is then
+                    simply right. Three earlier attempts published the extent as
+                    a property on /emu68 instead; none arrived, because
+                    dt_add_property() edits Emu68's in-memory tree and the guest
+                    receives a byte copy of the original blob.
+                */
+                if (base < host_mem_end)
+                {
+                    if (base + size <= host_mem_end)
+                    {
+                        kprintf("Below Emu68's own memory. Removing\n");
+                        for (int j = 0; j < size_cells + address_cells; j++)
+                            range[b * block_size + j] = 0;
+                        continue;
+                    }
+
+                    uintptr_t w;
+
+                    size -= host_mem_end - base;
+                    base = host_mem_end;
+                    kprintf("Raising past Emu68's own memory to %p - %p ",
+                            base, base + size - 1);
+
+                    w = base;
+                    for (int j = address_cells - 1; j >= 0; j--)
+                    {
+                        range[b * block_size + j] = w;
+                        w >>= 32;
+                    }
+                    w = size;
+                    for (int j = size_cells - 1; j >= 0; j--)
+                    {
+                        range[b * block_size + address_cells + j] = w;
+                        w >>= 32;
+                    }
+                }
 
                 if (base + size < top_of_ram)
                 {
